      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of different starting lineups possible with 5 players, given that two specific players, Ace and Zeppo, must be in the lineup, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Select the Players:**\n   We need to choose 3 more players from the remaining 10 (since Ace and Zeppo are already chosen).\n\n2. **Calculate the Combinations:**\n   The number of ways to choose 3 players out of 10 can be calculated using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{r} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( r \\) items from \\( n \\) items without regard to order.\n   \n   Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( r = 3 \\).\n\n   The combination formula is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n   \\]\n\n   Plugging in the values:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the Factorials:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3!}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\( 3! \\):**\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Divide by \\( 3! \\):**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6} = \\frac{720}{6} = 120\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting lineups possible is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
